# Lead Portal — environment variables.
#
# These are ALL of them. The app reads DATABASE_URL, the five SMTP_* variables,
# INGEST_TOKEN, RECORDINGS_DIR, SCREENSHOTS_DIR, the six SCREENSHOT_* settings,
# the three ACTIVITY_* settings, MONITOR_INSTALLER_PATH, MONITOR_VERSION,
# and NODE_ENV which Next sets itself
# (`grep -rn "process.env" app components lib` to confirm). There are no
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables, so nothing is inlined into the browser bundle and
# nothing here reaches a client.
#
# SESSIONS ADD NO VARIABLES, deliberately. They are rows in Postgres addressed
# by a random token, not signed blobs, so there is no signing secret to
# distribute, rotate or leak — and no bootstrap admin credentials, because an
# account that exists because the environment says so is an account every
# deployment shares. The first administrator is created with
# `npm run user:create` (see README).
#
# THE SECOND FACTOR DOES ADD VARIABLES: sign-in emails a six-digit code, and
# sending mail needs a mailbox. See the SMTP block below.
#
# NODE_ENV does affect authentication in two ways, both automatic:
#   * the session cookie is `Secure` and named `__Host-lp_session` in
#     production, plain `lp_session` in development (a `__Host-` cookie cannot
#     be set over plain http, so development could never sign in). The pending
#     OTP cookie follows the identical rule: `__Host-lp_otp` / `lp_otp`;
#   * Prisma logging drops to errors only.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT
#   cp .env.example .env.local     (.env.local is gitignored; this file is not)
#
# PRODUCTION
#   Do not use a file in the release directory. deploy/provision.sh writes
#   /etc/lead-portal/env, owned by root and mode 600, which systemd injects via
#   EnvironmentFile. The app user cannot read it, and it survives deploys
#   because it lives outside the release tree.

# --- Required --------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string.
#
# Local: create the database first — `createdb lead_portal`.
# If the password contains @ : / ? or #, percent-encode it (@ -> %40, # -> %23).
#
# Production adds `connection_limit`: Prisma's default pool is
# (physical_cpus * 2 + 1) per process, and with two blue/green slots briefly
# running at once that can exceed Postgres's default max_connections of 100.
#   postgresql://leadportal:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/lead_portal?schema=public&connection_limit=10
# For a hosted database, append `&sslmode=require`.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/lead_portal?schema=public"

# SMTP, for the sign-in verification code.
#
# REQUIRED. Signing in is password + a six-digit code emailed to the address on
# the user's record, and the login route refuses every attempt when these are
# unset (`isMailConfigured` in lib/mail.ts). That is deliberate: a deployment
# that forgot its mail settings must fail closed, not quietly fall back to
# password-only sessions.
#
# The mailbox is the Hostinger one the portal already owns. Port 465 is implicit
# TLS, which is what Hostinger publishes; the code derives `secure` from the
# port, so moving to 587 (STARTTLS) needs no code change.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. None of these has a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so Next inlines
# none of them into the browser bundle, and the only importers of lib/mail.ts
# are route handlers and lib/loginOtp.ts.
#
# NEVER COMMIT THE REAL PASSWORD. This file is in Git and is a template: leave
# SMTP_PASSWORD empty here. Put the real one in .env.local for development
# (gitignored) and in /etc/lead-portal/env for production, which is root-owned,
# mode 600, injected by systemd and outside the release tree.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.hostinger.com
SMTP_PORT=465
SMTP_USER=leads@spiderhunts-coworkingspace.com
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_FROM=leads@spiderhunts-coworkingspace.com


# --- Optional --------------------------------------------------------------

# Where uploaded call recordings are stored (lib/recordingStorage.ts). The
# audio is NOT in Postgres — only its metadata is, in `meeting_recordings`.
#
# Unset, it defaults to `.data/recordings` in the project directory, which is
# gitignored and fine for development.
#
# PRODUCTION MUST SET THIS, to a path outside the release tree. deploy.sh
# replaces the whole slot directory on every release, so a default-located
# recordings folder would be destroyed by the next deploy and a rollback could
# not bring it back. provision.sh writes
# RECORDINGS_DIR=/var/lib/leadportal/recordings and the systemd unit grants
# write access to exactly that path (ProtectSystem=strict makes everything else
# read-only).
#
# Nothing serves this directory: it is not under `public/`, nginx has no
# location for it, and the only way audio leaves the server is
# GET /api/meetings/:leadId/recording/stream, which checks the session first.
# RECORDINGS_DIR=/var/lib/leadportal/recordings

# Where desktop screenshots uploaded by SpiderHunts Monitor are stored
# (lib/screenshotStorage.ts). As with recordings the images are NOT in
# Postgres — only their metadata is, in the `screenshots` table.
#
# Unset, it defaults to `.data/screenshots` in the project directory, which is
# gitignored and fine for development.
#
# PRODUCTION MUST SET THIS, to a path outside the release tree, for the same
# reason RECORDINGS_DIR must: deploy.sh replaces the whole slot directory on
# every release, so a default-located screenshots folder would be destroyed by
# the next deploy and a rollback could not bring it back.
#
# Volume is what makes this different from recordings, and the difference is
# large: several images per agent per hour, all day, at roughly 160KB each. Put
# it on a filesystem with room to grow, and decide a retention policy before
# switching monitoring on for a whole team.
#
# Nothing serves this directory: it is not under `public/`, nginx has no
# location for it, and there is currently no route that reads it at all. The
# admin viewer will read it through an authenticated API in a later stage.
# SCREENSHOTS_DIR=/var/lib/leadportal/screenshots

# --- Screenshot cadence, rate limit and retention --------------------------
#
# THE SERVER OWNS THESE. An agent must not be able to decide how often their own
# screen is photographed or how long the images are kept, so none of them is
# configurable on the workstation: the capture interval is sent to the desktop
# client in the answer to GET /api/monitor/session, and everything else is
# enforced here. The client's built-in 10–30 minutes is a fallback for a portal
# it has not reached yet, not a setting.
#
# Every one of these falls back to its default and warns once if it is invalid
# (lib/screenshotPolicy.ts). A typo costs a log line, not a day of monitoring.

# How far apart the workstation's randomised screenshots are. It draws a fresh
# random delay in [min, max] before every capture — never a fixed interval, and
# never a capture at login. Defaults: 10 and 30.
#
# Validated as a pair: min >= 1 and max > min, or both fall back to the
# defaults. Setting only one of the two is fine; setting min above the default
# max is the case that falls back.
# SCREENSHOT_MIN_INTERVAL_MINUTES=10
# SCREENSHOT_MAX_INTERVAL_MINUTES=30

# The server-side brake on POST /api/monitor/screenshots: at most one ACCEPTED
# screenshot per monitor device per this many minutes, enforced against the
# server's clock and a column only the server writes
# (lib/screenshotRateLimit.ts). Nothing a workstation sends can move it.
#
# This is deliberately not derived from the interval above: it exists for the
# case where the uploader is not the client we shipped, so it must not follow a
# number that client is told. Default 5.
#
# BECAUSE it does not follow, lowering SCREENSHOT_MIN_INTERVAL_MINUTES without
# lowering this as well is the mistake to watch for: the workstation captures at
# the faster cadence and every upload arriving inside this window is refused
# with a 429. Keep it strictly below the minimum interval. At a 1-5 minute
# cadence, for instance, this must be under 1 — 0.5 is the floor the validator
# accepts.
# SCREENSHOT_UPLOAD_MIN_INTERVAL_MINUTES=5

# How long a screenshot is kept. Past this the nightly sweep deletes the file
# AND its database row (lib/screenshotRetention.ts). A whole number of days
# between 1 and 3650; anything else falls back to 30.
#
# Measured from the server-stamped `created_at`, never from the client's
# `captured_at` — otherwise a workstation could make its own screenshots
# eligible for deletion on arrival by lying about when they were taken.
# SCREENSHOT_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# --- Activity tracking -----------------------------------------------------
#
# THE SERVER OWNS THESE TOO, for the same reason and by the same mechanism as
# the screenshot settings above: an agent must not be able to decide how their
# own activity is measured. They are read on the server (lib/activityPolicy.ts),
# shipped to the workstation in the answer to GET /api/monitor/session, and
# enforced on the way in — POST /api/monitor/activity recomputes the percentage
# from the raw counts and discards whatever the client proposed.
#
# There is no UI to change them, deliberately. A settings screen would put these
# somewhere an application bug could write to; an environment variable in a
# root-owned /etc/leadportal/env cannot be reached from any request at all,
# whatever the caller's role.
#
# Each falls back to its default and warns once if it is invalid.

# How long one activity interval covers. The Monitor reports one per window, and
# the API refuses an interval longer than twice this. Whole seconds, 10–900.
# Default 60.
# ACTIVITY_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60

# How long without keyboard or mouse input before an employee is shown as
# inactive. Whole seconds, 60–14400. Default 300 (five minutes).
#
# THIS DOES NOT STOP ANYONE'S CLOCK. Work sessions remain the sole authority on
# when somebody was working; this only decides how the dashboard labels them and
# how tracked time is split into active and idle. An idle agent is still on the
# clock, because they are still at work.
# ACTIVITY_IDLE_THRESHOLD_SECONDS=300

# The denominator of the activity percentage: keyboard-plus-mouse events per
# minute that count as 100%. Whole number, 10–10000. Default 120.
#
#   observed   = keyboardActivityCount + mouseActivityCount
#   expected   = ACTIVITY_EXPECTED_EVENTS_PER_MINUTE * (durationSeconds / 60)
#   percentage = round(min(100, observed / expected * 100))
#
# IT IS NOT A PRODUCTIVITY MEASURE and must not be read as one. It counts input
# events and nothing else, so a fifteen-minute call that ends in a booked meeting
# scores near zero on it. Raising this number does not make anybody work harder;
# it makes everybody's percentage smaller.
#
# Nothing about what is typed, clicked or displayed is stored anywhere — only the
# two counts. See the note on `ActivityInterval` in prisma/schema.prisma.
# ACTIVITY_EXPECTED_EVENTS_PER_MINUTE=120

# Bearer token for POST /api/maintenance/screenshot-retention, the endpoint the
# box's cron calls once a night to run that sweep
# (deploy/leadportal-screenshot-retention).
#
# Leave it unset and the route refuses EVERY request with a 503 — it never falls
# open. The consequence of forgetting it is that screenshots are never deleted,
# which is visible as a growing SCREENSHOTS_DIR rather than as data loss.
#
# The endpoint takes no parameters at all, so the token buys exactly one power:
# "apply the configured retention window". There is no id, path or date to pass
# it, which is why no agent or admin can trigger arbitrary deletion.
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# Production: deploy/provision.sh generates it into /etc/leadportal/env, with a
# copy in /etc/leadportal/screenshot-retention-token.
# SCREENSHOT_RETENTION_TOKEN=

# --- SpiderHunts Monitor installer -----------------------------------------
#
# The Windows installer agents download from the portal (Account menu →
# Download Monitor, or /downloads). It is a file placed on the server by hand
# during deployment — there is no upload endpoint, and no request from any role
# can replace it or change the version.
#
# NOT A SECRET, and still behind authentication. `GET /api/downloads/monitor`
# resolves the session from Postgres before it opens the file, because an
# unauthenticated download URL would be the one route in this application that
# hands a stranger 77MB and a list of what this company monitors.

# Absolute path to the installer (lib/monitorRelease.ts).
#
# Unset, it defaults to `.data/downloads/<filename>` in the project directory,
# which is gitignored and fine for development: drop the exe there under the
# exact name below and the button works.
#
# PRODUCTION MUST SET THIS, to a path outside the release tree, for the same
# reason RECORDINGS_DIR and SCREENSHOTS_DIR must: deploy.sh replaces the whole
# slot directory on every release, so an installer stored inside it would be
# destroyed by the next deploy. deploy/provision.sh creates
# /var/lib/leadportal/downloads (owned by the app user, 0750) and writes this
# variable pointing into it. The systemd unit does NOT list that directory under
# ReadWritePaths, deliberately — the application only ever reads it, and
# ProtectSystem=strict leaves it readable while making it read-only.
#
# The path is never echoed to a client, in any response, in success or failure.
# Nothing about it comes from a request either: the route takes no parameter at
# all, so there is no filename to point elsewhere and nothing to traverse with.
# MONITOR_INSTALLER_PATH=/var/lib/leadportal/downloads/SpiderHunts-Monitor-Windows-0.1.0-Setup.exe

# Which version that file is. Defaults to the version in lib/monitorRelease.ts
# (0.1.0) and is only needed when the installer on the box is newer than the
# portal release serving it.
#
# It is the single source of the version *and* of the filename: the name the
# browser saves is derived from it as
# `SpiderHunts-Monitor-Windows-<version>-Setup.exe`, so the two can never
# disagree. `x.y.z`, optionally with a pre-release tail; anything else warns
# once and falls back rather than putting "Version undefined" on an agent's
# screen. Set it together with MONITOR_INSTALLER_PATH when the installer is
# replaced.
# MONITOR_VERSION=0.1.0


# --- Development only ------------------------------------------------------
# IGNORED unless NODE_ENV is something other than `production`, so no
# combination of variables on a real deployment can produce a ten-second capture
# loop. They exist so the scheduler can be tested in a minute instead of an
# hour; set all three together, or the rate limit will 429 every capture but the
# first and the test will prove nothing.
# SCREENSHOT_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS=10
# SCREENSHOT_MAX_INTERVAL_SECONDS=20
# SCREENSHOT_UPLOAD_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS=5

# Shared secret for POST /api/leads/ingest — the endpoint the Yelp scraper (a
# separate project, in a separate directory) pushes its output folder to. The
# scraper sends it as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
#
# Leave it unset and the route rejects EVERY request with a 503. It never falls
# open: an ingest endpoint that accepts anonymous writes because a deploy forgot
# a variable is the exact thing the token exists to prevent. Nothing else in the
# app is affected — the browser Import view uses /api/leads/upload and needs no
# token.
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# Production: deploy/provision.sh generates it into /etc/leadportal/env and
# prints it once; a copy stays in /etc/leadportal/ingest-token.
# INGEST_TOKEN=


# --- Set by the platform, listed here so the full set is visible -----------

# `next build` and the standalone server set this themselves; systemd also sets
# it explicitly in /etc/lead-portal/env. Never set it in .env.local — doing so
# makes `next dev` behave as production and silently disables hot reload.
# NODE_ENV=production

# Which interface the standalone server binds to. Production pins this to
# loopback so nginx is the only route in and the Basic Auth in front of it
# cannot be bypassed by connecting to the port directly. Unset in development,
# where Next binds all interfaces for device testing.
# HOSTNAME=127.0.0.1

# Port for the standalone server. Set per blue/green slot on the VPS
# (/etc/lead-portal/slot-blue.env = 3001, slot-green.env = 3002).
# Development uses Next's default 3000.
# PORT=3001
